## Root cause chain (with file:line evidence)

### 1. Where the WARN is generated

`fs/kernfs/dir.c:1724-1734`:
```c
int kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(struct kernfs_node *parent, const char *name, ...)
{
	if (!parent) {
		WARN(1, KERN_WARNING "kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\n", name);
		return -ENOENT;
	}
```
The WARN fires **only when `parent == NULL`** — not when the name is merely absent (a missing name takes the `kn == NULL` path at `dir.c:1739-1751` and returns `-ENOENT` silently).

`fs/sysfs/symlink.c:143-153`:
```c
void sysfs_remove_link(struct kobject *kobj, const char *name)
{
	struct kernfs_node *parent = NULL;
	if (!kobj) parent = sysfs_root_kn;
	else       parent = kobj->sd;      /* <-- NULL after device_del() */
	kernfs_remove_by_name(parent, name);
}
```
So `parent == NULL` ⇔ **`kobj->sd == NULL`** ⇔ the `struct device` whose kobj is passed has already been through `device_del()` (or was never `device_add()`ed).

### 2. Who passes the dead kobj with name "typec"

Only one site in the tree creates/removes a link literally named `"typec"`:

`drivers/usb/typec/class.c:888-904` (create) and `drivers/usb/typec/class.c:906-913` (remove):
```c
static void typec_partner_unlink_device(struct typec_partner *partner, struct device *dev)
{
	sysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, dev_name(dev));
	sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec");   /* class.c:909  <-- the WARN */
	if (partner->deattach)
		partner->deattach(partner, dev);
}
```
Here `dev` is a **USB device** (`port->usb2_dev` / `port->usb3_dev`), *not* a typec device.

### 3. The offending caller in the crash path

`drivers/usb/typec/class.c:1174-1195`:
```c
void typec_unregister_partner(struct typec_partner *partner)
{
	...
	mutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock);
	if (port->usb2_dev) {                                  /* 1183 */
		typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb2_dev);  /* 1184 */
		port->usb2_dev = NULL;
	}
	if (port->usb3_dev) {                                  /* 1188 */
		typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb3_dev);  /* 1189 */
		port->usb3_dev = NULL;
	}
	device_unregister(&partner->dev);
	mutex_unlock(&port->partner_link_lock);
}
```
Reached from:
- `drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1138-1154` `ucsi_unregister_partner()` → `typec_unregister_partner(con->partner)` at **ucsi.c:1151**
- `drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1264-1364` `ucsi_handle_connector_change()` (the `events` workqueue item, `con->work`) → `ucsi_unregister_partner(con)` at **ucsi.c:1325** in the `CONNECT_CHANGE && !CONNECTED` branch.

Exactly matching the reported stack: `kworker/6:0 → ucsi_handle_connector_change → ucsi_unregister_partner → typec_unregister_partner → typec_partner_unlink_device → sysfs_remove_link → kernfs_remove_by_name_ns`.

### 4. Why `port->usb2_dev/usb3_dev` points at a *dead* USB device

The typec port caches raw `struct device *` pointers to USB devices, **without taking a reference**:

`drivers/usb/typec/class.h:78-85`:
```c
	/* NOTE: These may be registered first before the typec_partner, so they
	 * will always have to be kept here instead of struct typec_partner. */
	struct device *usb2_dev;   /* class.h:84 */
	struct device *usb3_dev;   /* class.h:85 */
```
Set in `typec_partner_attach()` (`class.c:2144-2167`, assignments at 2151/2154) — note the pointer is stored **even when no partner exists yet** (`typec_get_partner()` may return NULL, `class.c:2132-2142`), and no `get_device()` is ever taken.

The two producers/consumers of those pointers are:

* `drivers/usb/core/hub.c:2642-2737` `usb_new_device()` → `typec_attach(port_dev->connector, &udev->dev)` at **hub.c:2722** (and `drivers/usb/core/port.c:695-720` `connector_bind()` → `typec_attach()` at **port.c:717** for an already-present child).
* `drivers/usb/core/hub.c:2315-2394` `usb_disconnect()` → `typec_deattach(port_dev->connector, &udev->dev)` at **hub.c:2363**, immediately followed by `device_del(&udev->dev)` at **hub.c:2376**.

`typec_attach/typec_deattach` are conditional on `con` being non-NULL — `include/linux/usb/typec.h:404-408` and `410-414`:
```c
static inline void typec_deattach(struct typec_connector *con, struct device *dev)
{
	if (con && con->deattach) con->deattach(con, dev);
}
```

**The hole:** `drivers/usb/core/port.c:722-729`
```c
static void connector_unbind(struct device *dev, struct device *connector, void *data)
{
	sysfs_remove_link(&connector->kobj, dev_name(dev));
	sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "connector");
	port_dev->connector = NULL;          /* port.c:728 */
}
```
`connector_unbind()` drops `port_dev->connector` **without calling `typec_deattach()`**. From that moment:

1. `usb_disconnect()` sees `port_dev->connector == NULL` at hub.c:2363 → `typec_partner_deattach()` (`class.c:2169-2186`) never runs → `port->usb2_dev/usb3_dev` are **never cleared** and never validated.
2. `device_del(&udev->dev)` (hub.c:2376) nulls `udev->dev.kobj.sd`; `put_device()` at hub.c:2393 may even free it.
3. Later the UCSI event work runs `ucsi_unregister_partner()` → `typec_unregister_partner()`, sees the stale non-NULL `port->usb2_dev` (class.c:1183) and calls `sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec")` on a device whose `kobj->sd` is NULL → `parent == NULL` → the WARN at `fs/kernfs/dir.c:1731`.

At boot on this machine the trigger is the usual UCSI/Thunderbolt-dock sequence: connector↔usb-port component links are (re)bound/unbound while UCSI is still replaying `CONNECT_CHANGE` events queued from `ucsi_init()` (`ucsi.c:1833-1931`, `ucsi_connector_change()` kick at **ucsi.c:1902-1903**), so the unbind/disconnect and the `con->work` handler interleave. Same reachable via `ucsi_check_connection()` → `ucsi_unregister_partner()` at **ucsi.c:1221** and `ucsi_unregister()` at **ucsi.c:2147**, and the `ucsi_init()` error path at **ucsi.c:1912**.

